<edge source="global_finance_shock::sovereign_fiscal_pressur" target="china_tech_pressure::state_policy_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising sovereign fiscal pressure forces authorities to increase administrative interventions, directed credit and industrial support (to stabilize revenues, employment and strategic firms), thereby raising overall state-directed policy intensity.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_finance_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">china_tech_pressure</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_finance_shock::sovereign_fiscal_pressur" target="democratic_unrest_wave::protest_event_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Acute sovereign fiscal pressure forces rapid subsidy cuts, delayed public wages or new consumption taxes that produce immediate material grievances and trigger organized protests and strikes.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_finance_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">democratic_unrest_wave</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_finance_shock::sovereign_fiscal_pressur" target="russia_ukraine_war::satcom_availability" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising fiscal pressure in donor and allied states reduces budgetary allocations and ad‑hoc purchases of commercial satellite capacity (including subsidised wartime bandwidth), lowering the net available satcom capacity for actors in the theatre.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_finance_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_finance_shock::sovereign_fiscal_pressur" target="russia_ukraine_war::state_pressure_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating fiscal stress motivates governments to extract concessions from private providers (via regulatory demands, procurement conditions, or diplomatic pressure) to secure communications or revenue, increasing the intensity and persistence of state pressure on operators.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_finance_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_finance_shock::sovereign_fiscal_pressur" target="us_iran_standoff::proxy_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A global finance shock that raises sovereign fiscal pressure in Iran (or its patrons) forces budget cuts and delays in external subsidy/arms transfers, directly reducing the funding, logistics and munitions available to proxy groups and thus lowering their operational tempo.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_finance_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_standoff</data>
</edge>
